.Dd September 2, 2026
.Dt NG_MODBUS 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ng_modbus
.Nd Modbus protocol engine in netgraph
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm kldload
.Ar modbus
.Nm ngctl
.Ar create
.Ar modbus
.Nm ngctl
.Ar config
.Ar modbus
.Cm setconfig role= Ns Ar N transport= Ns Ar N unit_id= Ns Ar N
.Nm ngctl
.Ar config
.Ar modbus
.Cm setbackend ncoils= Ns Ar N ndiscrete_in= Ns Ar N ninput_regs= Ns Ar N
.Ar nholding_regs Ar = Ns Ar N
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
implements the Modbus protocol as a server or a client.
Each node is an independent endpoint with its own register map, its own parser
and, as a client, its own outstanding transaction.
.Pp
The implementation is a transport neutral core with a framing layer around it.
A framing turns the bytes of a link into request and response PDUs; the core
decodes a request, executes it against a register map and builds the response.
Nothing in the core or in the function code handlers knows which framing
delivered the request, so one implementation serves TCP, a serial line and a
test harness alike.
.Pp
A link carries a byte stream, not messages, so each byte is fed through the
framing in order.
The MBAP header of
.Cm transport=TCP
delimits a frame and carries its transaction identifier;
.Cm transport=RTU
has no length field, so a frame ends when its CRC verifies and 1.5 character
times of silence discards one that was cut short;
.Cm transport=ASCII
is delimited by a colon and CR LF and checked with an LRC;
.Cm transport=NONE
and
.Cm transport=VNET
carry exactly one bare PDU per item.
Responses are framed the same way their request arrived.
.Pp
The data model is the one the specification defines: coils and holding
registers are readable and writable, discrete inputs and input registers are
read only, and all four are 16 bit addressable.
.Pp
The node has one hook,
.Li lower ,
which carries the framed bytes described by
.Cm transport ;
control messages go to the node itself and need no hook.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Bl -tag -width "setconfig" -compact
.It Cm setconfig role= Ns Ar N transport= Ns Ar N unit_id= Ns Ar N
.Cm role
is 1 for a server, which answers requests, or 2 for a client, which issues
them.
.Cm transport
is 0 or 1 for a bare PDU, 2 for TCP, 3 for RTU or 4 for ASCII.
.Cm unit_id
is this node's address, 1 to 247, or 0 for broadcast; the Serial Line guide
reserves 248 to 255, so identifiers outside that range are refused.
.It Cm getconfig
Return the current configuration.
.It Cm setbackend ncoils= Ns Ar N ndiscrete_in= Ns Ar N ninput_regs= Ns Ar N nholding_regs Ar = Ns Ar N
Resize the four areas, each between 1 and 4096.
A refused size leaves the existing map untouched; a resize does not preserve
contents.
.It Cm getbackend
Return the current sizes.
.It Cm request pdu= Ns Ar N pdulen= Ns Ar N unit_id= Ns Ar N tid= Ns Ar N
Issue one request as a client.
.Cm pdu
is the request PDU without framing.
It is refused while another request is outstanding, or when the
.Li lower
hook has no peer.
.It Cm getresponse
Return the state of the last transaction, one of idle, pending, done, expired
or failed, and the response PDU when one was accepted.
.It Cm getinfo
Return the ABI version, the maximum PDU and ADU sizes, the supported framings
and the serial line parameters.
.It Cm getstats , Cm clrstats
Return or zero the counters.
.El
.Pp
Control messages carry fixed width fields and a version, and never expose an
internal structure or a kernel address.
A request for another unit identifier is ignored; a broadcast request is
executed but never answered.
.Sh FUNCTION CODES
1 (0x01) read coils, 2 (0x02) read discrete inputs, 3 (0x03) read holding
registers, 4 (0x04) read input registers, 5 (0x05) write single coil, 6 (0x06)
write single register, 15 (0x0F) write multiple coils, 16 (0x10) write multiple
registers, 22 (0x16) mask write register and 23 (0x17) read/write multiple
registers.
.Pp
Each enforces the quantity and byte count limits of its request diagram.
An unsupported function code is answered with exception 1, an inconsistent
length, quantity or byte count with exception 3, and an address outside the
configured map with exception 2.
An exception is a response on the wire, never a kernel error.
.Sh STATISTICS
.Cm getstats
reports items and bytes received, frames and bytes transmitted, errors, and
expired client transactions.
Errors are counted rather than logged, so diagnostics cannot become a timing
problem in the data path.
.Sh CAVEATS
The client issues one request at a time and never retries; a transaction with
no usable response within
.Dv MODBUS_CLIENT_TIMEOUT_MS
milliseconds is given up.
.Pp
There is no object dictionary, so no parameter can be discovered or changed
through SDO and the device identity is fixed.
The register map is zeroed when a node is created and has no persistence or
access control.
.Pp
No serial driver offers RS-485 direction control, so a half duplex line needs
a transceiver that controls direction itself.
Launch time transmission is not implemented; the fields the specification
defines are kept for a driver that can schedule a transmission itself, but a
frame is always sent as soon as the cycle reaches it.
.Pp
RTU framing depends on silence, and a software timer is not exact: above
19200 Bd the 3.5 character gap of 1.750 ms can be missed or measured late.
No real-time guarantee is made.
.Sh STANDARDS
Behaviour is defined by three documents from the Modbus Organization, published
at
.Li https://modbus.org .
The MODBUS Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3 defines the data model,
the function codes, the exception codes and the request and response layouts.
The MODBUS over Serial Line Specification and Implementation Guide V1.02
defines the unit identifier ranges, the RTU frame with its CRC, the character
times that delimit it, and the ASCII frame with its LRC.
The MODBUS Messaging on TCP/IP Implementation Guide V1.0b defines the MBAP
header and the meaning of its length field.
